What it is: A compact Field Service Management training app called ServiceForge — a realistic boilerplate for building features like technician scheduling, work orders, and SLA tracking.

Tech stack (simple):

Backend: Java 17 + Spring Boot (in-memory mock data, no real DB). See ServiceForgeApplication.java.
Frontend: Angular SPA that calls the backend REST API. See main.ts and app.module.ts.
Purpose and constraints:

Training / demo codebase — safe to modify and extend.
No external services or real databases — all data lives in MockDataStore and is seeded at startup.
Focus on clear feature pipelines and conventions rather than production hardening.
One-rule workflow:

Every new feature must follow the BA → Developer → Tester pipeline described in orchestration.md. Read that before starting a feature.
Project conventions to remember:

Backend: package-by-layer (model, data, service, controller), constructor injection only, controllers return ResponseEntity<T>.
Frontend: HTTP calls centralized in services, components only render and delegate.
Validation and error handling use ApiError DTOs.
How to run (quick):

Backend: mvn spring-boot:run (from /backend)
Frontend: npm install && npm start (from /frontend)
Where feature specs and decisions live:

Feature specs: pipeline/features/ (example: feature-1-technician-availability.md)
Decisions recorded per feature: pipeline/decisions/ (example: feature-1-decisions.md)
Where to start reading:

Project entry & rules: AGENTS.md
Pipeline & roles: orchestration.md
Backend mock data: MockDataStore under backend data/
Example feature implementation: technician availability controllers and services under backend controller/ and service/, and the matching frontend component in technician-calendar.
Quick checklist for contributing:

Read AGENTS.md and orchestration.md.
Create a single feature spec under pipeline/features/.
Implement following conventions (mock data, DI, services).
Record any long-lived decisions under pipeline/decisions/.
